  "text": "We are given a complex number:\n\n$$\nn = \\frac{(13 + 7i)^4}{(5 + 2i)^4}.\n$$\n\nLet $ a $ be the **smallest prime divisor** of $ n $. We are asked to determine whether there exists an integer power $ k $ of $ a $ such that:\n\n- $ a^k \\mid \\Re(n) $\n- $ a^k \\mid \\Im(n) $\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Compute $ n $\n\nWe can simplify $ n $ by first computing the magnitude and argument of each complex number.\n\nLet’s compute $ 13 + 7i $ and $ 5 + 2i $:\n\n- $ |13 + 7i| = \\sqrt{13^2 + 7^2} = \\sqrt{169 + 49} = \\sqrt{218} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 109} $\n- $ |5 + 2i| = \\sqrt{5^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{25 + 4} = \\sqrt{29} $\n\nSo the magnitude of $ n $ is:\n\n$$\n|n| = \\frac{|\\text{numerator}|}{|\\text{denominator}|} = \\frac{\\sqrt{218}}{\\sqrt{29}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{218}{29}} = \\sqrt{7.5}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the **real and imaginary parts**, we can use the fact that:\n\n$$\n(13 + 7i)^4 = |13 + 7i|^4 \\cdot e^{i \\cdot 4 \\arg(13 + 7i)}\n$$\n$$\n(5 + 2i)^4 = |5 + 2i|^4 \\cdot e^{i \\cdot 4 \\arg(5 + 2i)}\n$$\n\nLet’s compute $ \\arg(13 + 7i) $ and $ \\arg(5 + 2i) $.\n\n- $ \\arg(13 + 7i) = \\tan^{-1}(7/13) $\n- $ \\arg(5 + 2i) = \\tan^{-1}(2/5) $\n\nSo:\n\n- $ \\arg(13 + 7i) = \\theta_1 = \\tan^{-1}(7/13) $\n- $ \\arg(5 + 2i) = \\theta_2 = \\tan^{-1}(2/5) $\n\nTherefore:\n\n- $ (13 + 7i)^4 = (218)^2 e^{i 4 \\theta_1} $\n- $ (5 + 2i)^4 = (29)^2 e^{i 4 \\theta_2} $\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nn = \\frac{(218)^2 e^{i 4 \\theta_1}}{(29)^2 e^{i 4 \\theta_2}} = \\frac{218^2}{29^2} \\cdot \\frac{e^{i 4 (\\theta_1 - \\theta_2)}}{\\cos(4 \\theta_2) + i \\sin(4 \\theta_2)}\n$$\n\nNow compute $ 4 \\theta_1 - 4 \\theta_2 = 4(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2) $\n\nWe can compute this angle numerically if needed.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the prime factorization of $ n $\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n$$\nn = \\left( \\frac{218}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot e^{i \\phi}\n$$\n\nLet’s compute $ \\frac{218}{29} $:\n\n- $ 218 = 2 \\cdot 109 $, and 29 is a prime number.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\frac{218}{29} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{109}{29}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\nn = \\left( 2 \\cdot \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot e^{i \\phi}\n= 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot e^{i \\phi}\n$$\n\nSo $ n = 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot e^{i \\phi} $\n\nNow, the **real and imaginary parts** of $ n $ are:\n\n- $ \\text{Re}(n) = 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot \\cos(\\phi) $\n- $ \\text{Im}(n) = 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot \\sin(\\phi) $\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the smallest prime divisor of $ n $\n\nWe have:\n\n$$\nn = 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot e^{i \\phi}\n$$\n\nSo $ n $ has a **factor of 4** and $ \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 $, where 109 is prime and 29 is prime.\n\nTherefore, the **prime factors** of $ n $ are:\n\n- 2\n- 109\n- 29\n\nSo the **smallest prime divisor** of $ n $ is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Check if there exists an integer power of $ a = 2 $ dividing both $ \\text{Re}(n) $ and $ \\text{Im}(n) $\n\nLet’s compute $ \\text{Re}(n) $ and $ \\text{Im}(n) $:\n\n$$\n\\text{Re}(n) = 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot \\cos(\\phi)\n$$\n$$\n\\text{Im}(n) = 4 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 \\cdot \\sin(\\phi)\n$$\n\nWe want to find an integer $ k $ such that:\n\n$$\n2^k \\mid \\text{Re}(n) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2^k \\mid \\text{Im}(n)\n$$\n\nSince $ \\text{Re}(n) $ and $ \\text{Im}(n) $ are both multiples of $ 2^k \\cdot \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 $, and since $ \\frac{109}{29} $ is irrational, we must have:\n\n- $ 2^k $ divides both $ \\text{Re}(n) $ and $ \\text{Im}(n) $, and\n- $ \\left( \\frac{109}{29} \\right)^2 $ is not rational.\n\nSo the **only common divisor** is $ 2^k $, and since $ \\frac{109}{29} $ is irrational, there is **no integer power of 2** that divides both $ \\text{Re}(n) $ and $ \\text{Im}(n) $.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$\n\nand the power is $ \\boxed{1} $, since $ 2^1 = 2 $ divides both $ \\text{Re}(n) $ and $ \\text{Im}(n) $.",
